     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet. As written, it adds up to a daily dose that's too high, so I need you to confirm what you meant.\n\n**The problem:**\n- 1700 mg three times a day comes to **5100 mg/day**.\n- The highest labeled dose of immediate-release metformin is **2550 mg/day**, so this is double that. For extended-release, the maximum is 2000 mg/day.\n- Doses this high raise the risk of lactic acidosis and serious stomach and bowel side effects, even when kidney and liver function are normal.\n\n**What you may have meant:**\n1. **850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals**, which is 2550 mg/day, the maximum dose.\n2. **1700 mg/day total**, given either as 850 mg twice daily with meals or as about 567 mg three times daily. In practice, 500 mg three times daily or 850 mg twice daily is more common.\n3. **1000 mg by mouth twice daily with meals**, which is 2000 mg/day, a common maintenance dose.\n\nAlso, is this a new start or does the patient already take metformin? New starts usually begin at 500 mg once or twice daily and go up slowly so the stomach can adjust.\n\nTell me which dose you want and I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
